#!/bin/bash

build() {
    # mkinitcpio does not source the helpers when the hook is run standalone
    declare -F tinyssh_add_server >/dev/null || . /usr/lib/initcpio/functions.d/systemd-extras

    tinyssh_add_base || return

    local variant="${SD_TINYSSH:-openssh-tpm2}"
    local openssh_host_key=/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key
    local keydir key_source converted= rc=0
    local exec_start credentials=()

    # the key pair in cleartext, either tinyssh's own or converted from the OpenSSH host key
    case "$variant" in

        openssh-tpm2)
            keydir="$(tinyssh_convert_openssh_key "$openssh_host_key")" || return
            key_source="OpenSSH host key '$openssh_host_key'"
            converted=1
            ;;

        tinykey-tpm2|tinykey-cleartext)
            keydir="$(tinyssh_key_directory)" || return
            key_source="tinyssh key pair in '$keydir'"
            ;;

        *)
            error "Unknown variant SD_TINYSSH='%s', pick %s, %s or %s" "$variant" \
                "openssh-tpm2" "tinykey-tpm2" "tinykey-cleartext"
            return 1
            ;;

    esac

    msg "variant %s, host key taken from the %s" "$variant" "$key_source"

    case "$variant" in

        *-tpm2)
            tinyssh_add_sealed_key "$keydir/ed25519.pk" "$keydir/.ed25519.sk"
            rc=$?
            exec_start="ExecStart=-/usr/bin/tinyssh-start $(tinyssh_command_option)"
            ;;

        *-cleartext)
            tinyssh_add_cleartext_key "$keydir/ed25519.pk" "$keydir/.ed25519.sk"
            rc=$?
            exec_start="ExecStart=-/usr/bin/tinysshd $(tinyssh_command_option)\${CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY}"
            credentials=( "LoadCredential=ed25519.pk" "LoadCredential=.ed25519.sk" )
            ;;

    esac

    [[ -n "$converted" ]] && rm -rf -- "${keydir%/*}"
    (( rc == 0 )) || return 1

    tinyssh_add_server "ExecStart=" "$exec_start" "${credentials[@]}"
}

help() {
    cat <<__EOF_HELP__
This hook enables tinyssh during the initramfs phase. It is only applicable for
a systemd based intramfs phase, i.e. hook 'systemd' must be given in the HOOKS
array in mkinitcpio.conf.

See https://github.com/wolegis/mkinitcpio-systemd-extras/wiki/TinySSH-server
for details.

The hook copies all files and binaries required by tinyssh to the initramfs
image and enables listening TCP port 22 (or some other).

Set SD_TINYSSH to choose which host key is served and how it is protected:

  openssh-tpm2       the OpenSSH host key of this system, sealed to the TPM,
                     used when SD_TINYSSH is not set at all
  tinykey-tpm2       a tinyssh key pair of its own, sealed to the TPM
  tinykey-cleartext  a tinyssh key pair of its own, stored in cleartext, the
                     only variant that works without a TPM

'openssh-tpm2' converts /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key on the fly. Nothing has
to be prepared, and the initramfs answers with the same host key as the booted
system, so the clients need no second entry in their known_hosts file. The
conversion requires python to be installed.

The 'tinykey' variants expect a key pair in \$SD_TINYSSH_KEYDIR (default
/etc/tinyssh/sshkeydir). It has to be generated first, otherwise the build
fails:

  tinysshd-makekey /etc/tinyssh/sshkeydir

The 'tpm2' variants seal the secret key while the image is built, so it is of
no use to anyone who reads the unencrypted initramfs. The build fails if no
usable TPM2 device is present. Sealing happens on every rebuild against the PCR
values of the moment, which repairs the host key after a firmware update: boot
once, unlock the volume, and the next 'mkinitcpio' run makes the image work
again.

Set SD_TINYSSH_TPM2_PCRS to the PCRs to seal against, e.g. "0+7". If it is
unset, the default of systemd-creds is used, which is PCR 7 alone.

At boot /usr/bin/tinyssh-start unseals the key itself instead of leaving that
to systemd, because a credential that cannot be decrypted fails its unit before
the server is even started. Should the unsealing fail, the script generates a
throwaway host key and reports its fingerprint on the console and in the
journal, so that a volume can still be unlocked from remote. The ssh client
reports a changed host key then and refuses to connect until the old key is
removed from its known_hosts file, e.g. with 'ssh-keygen -R <host>'.

'tinykey-cleartext' keeps the secret key unprotected. The initramfs is
unencrypted, so anyone who can read the image obtains the key and can
impersonate this machine.

By default tinyssh presents the busybox shell after successful login. Two
variables can be used to alter this behavior:

SD_TINYSSH_COMMAND defines a shell command. The string is appended to 'sh -c',
i.e. it may contain blanks, quotes and all kinds of special characters that are
interpreted by the shell.  Example: To allow to unlock LUKS encrypted volumes
from remote (but nothing else, especially no shell access) set:
SD_TINYSSH_COMMAND="systemd-tty-ask-password-agent --query --watch"

SD_TINYSSH_SCRIPT defines a file with shell commands that are executed before
the interactive shell is presented. Example: To allow to unlock LUKS encrypted
volumes from remote but also escape to an interactive shell with CTRL-C specify
a file that contains:
systemd-tty-ask-password-agent --query --watch

SD_TINYSSH_COMMAND takes precedence.

Set SD_TINYSSH_PORT to specify a port other than 22.

Login is allowed for the public keys taken from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys of
this system, which are copied into the image. tinyssh speaks ED25519 only, so
entries of every other key type are dropped and at least one 'ssh-ed25519'
entry has to be present. Set SD_TINYSSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS to read the keys from
another file.

If required the above mentioned variables must be set in /etc/mkinitcpio.conf.
__EOF_HELP__
}
